i":null}],"n_lists":1}},{"id":15942182184844,"type":"album","name":"The End of the Universe","artist":"Lewis Black","year":2002,"weight":3,"yt":"Mb7qDfIzQRk","src":"rym","from":{"books":[],"podcasts":[],"lists":[{"id":41025334127070,"name":"RYM Top — Spoken Word — Stand-Up Comedy","rank":7,"scaruffi":null}],"n_lists":1}}],"podcast":[{"kind":"podcast","title":"Evolution in the 70s","body":"While stand-up comedy was not a 70s invention, having been a staple of early 20th-century radio and TV shows like \"Milton Berle\" and \"Ed Sullivan,\" the decade saw its \"rock and rollization.\" This evolution involved becoming \"smarter and more current and edgier,\" often complete with drugs and alcohol, and led to an explosion of comedy clubs and mega-stars. \"The other thing that happens with comedy in the 1970s is it gets kind of I'm going to make something up here rock and rollized, complete with drugs and alcohol.\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"For the Record - The 70s","episode":"Ep. 41 - The Impact of The Troubadour on 1970s Popular Culture","url":null}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"Limited Comedy Clubs Prior to 70s","body":"Before and during the early 1970s, dedicated comedy clubs were not as prevalent as they would become in subsequent decades. This meant that venues like The Troubadour often featured comedy alongside music acts. \"Now, you have to keep in mind that comedy clubs weren't exactly a thing. I mean, I'm not saying there were none, but certainly not as many as that there will be after the 1970s and into the 1980s and 1990s.\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"For the Record - The 70s","episode":"Ep. 41 - The Impact of The Troubadour on 1970s Popular Culture","url":null}]}]}